    "result_text": "I've added the summary panel to the screen, and `bin/e2e` passes.\n\n**What it shows** (`features/summary/summary.ts`, placed under the Periods section):\n- **Per grade (A, B, C):** how many people are on that grade today, leaving out retired people, and their due-totals added up. The due-totals are the same figures the person rows show.\n- **Per period:** its due-total. A closed period shows the total fixed when it was closed, exactly as the Periods list does.\n- **Per status:** how many claims have that status. This counts every claim on the sheet, including those of retired or held people. Otherwise the counts couldn't match the list (in the test, the retired person's settled and voided claims are expected in the counts).\n\n**How the figures stay in agreement with the list:** nothing is recalculated a second time. The panel is handed the people list's own per-person due-totals. I moved the period due-total calculation out of the Periods component into two shared functions, and both the Periods list and the summary now use them. The server and stored data didn't need to change; everything the panel needs was already loaded on the screen.\n\n**Decisions for you:**\n- **PENDING status:** the test expects a count for a status called PENDING, but the app has no such status (claims are only DRAFT, OPEN, SETTLED or VOID). The panel shows a PENDING row that will always read 0.\n- **Who counts as retired:** anyone with a retirement date is excluded, even if that date is still in the future. This matches the \"Retired\" column in the people list, which shows \"yes\" as soon as a date is set.",
